    <h2>Final Recommendation</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>For most individual users in 2026, Google Drive is the default pick.</strong> The 15 GB free tier covers personal documents and photos without paying, Google Docs collaboration is the best in the industry, and search is unmatched. If you only need one cloud storage service and you do not need desktop Office, Google Drive covers 80% of consumer use cases.</p>
      <p><strong>For Windows users who already want Microsoft Office, OneDrive with Microsoft 365 Personal at $6.99 per month is the smartest bundle.</strong> You get 1 TB of storage plus desktop Word, Excel, and PowerPoint at a price cheaper than buying Office separately. The native Windows File Explorer integration is the cleanest desktop experience on Windows, and Personal Vault adds a security layer Google Drive and Dropbox lack.</p>
      <p><strong>For cross-platform users, Mac users, and anyone editing large files, Dropbox Plus at $11.99 per month remains worth the premium.</strong> Block-level sync on all platforms, LAN sync, 180-day version history, and the best Mac experience are features neither Google Drive nor OneDrive can match. If you work with video, design files, or databases that change frequently, Dropbox is the only service that will not waste bandwidth on full file re-uploads.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> Microsoft 365 Personal (1 TB OneDrive plus desktop Office) for $6.99/month, plus the free 15 GB Google Drive tier for collaboration and Google Photos, covers nearly every personal cloud storage scenario for under $7 per month. Add Dropbox Plus only if you specifically need its sync engine for large creative work.</p>
