    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Which is best for free storage: Google Drive, Dropbox, or OneDrive?</h3>
    <p>Google Drive is the best free option with 15 GB shared across Drive, Gmail, and Google Photos. OneDrive offers 5 GB free with a Microsoft account, and Dropbox only 2 GB. If you want the most free space without paying, Google Drive wins by a wide margin.</p>

    <h3>Is OneDrive included with Microsoft 365?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Microsoft 365 Personal ($6.99/month) includes 1 TB OneDrive storage plus desktop Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Microsoft 365 Family ($9.99/month) includes 1 TB per person for up to 6 people. This makes OneDrive the best value per TB if you already want Office apps.</p>

    <h3>Does Google Drive scan your files?</h3>
    <p>Google scans content stored in Drive for malware in shared files, copyright violations, and to power features like search and optical character recognition. Google's privacy policy allows automated content analysis for product features. Dropbox states it does not scan personal files for advertising or training purposes, and OneDrive business plans add data loss prevention policies.</p>

    <h3>Which has better file sync: Dropbox or OneDrive?</h3>
    <p>Dropbox has the strongest sync engine overall. It uses block-level sync on all operating systems, supports LAN sync between computers on the same network, and has the most reliable conflict resolution UI. OneDrive matches block-level sync on Windows but is weaker on Mac. For cross-platform reliability, Dropbox is the winner.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Google Drive, Dropbox, and OneDrive together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, many users run all three. A common setup is Microsoft 365 Personal for Office apps plus 1 TB OneDrive storage, plus the free 15 GB Google Drive tier for collaboration and Google Photos. Dropbox is added when you need the best sync for large creative files. The combined cost is around $6.99/month and covers nearly every personal cloud scenario.</p>

    <h3>Which cloud storage is best for Mac users?</h3>
    <p>Dropbox is the best cloud storage for Mac users. Its macOS app integrates cleanly with Finder, large file uploads are more reliable than OneDrive or Google Drive, and Smart Sync lets you see all files without using local storage. Google Drive is a close second for collaboration features, while OneDrive on Mac still lags behind its Windows counterpart.</p>

